# Action Links

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Use action links for a short group of useful destinations on a dashboard, such as “Housework” or “Heating and hot water”. Each link has an underlined name and a decorative arrow in a soft circle. The group has no card box and no heading of its own, so place it beneath the dashboard’s own heading.

A link navigates. A [button](buttons.html) operates a device or submits a change. This card never calls a service, changes a device or opens a pop-up. For a single link back to the previous page, use a [back link](back-link.html).

This page covers:

- Action Links
  - `civica-action-links`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

The card is a list of links. Each link has:

- a name, shown in bold underlined text,
- an arrow in a round tinted circle, which is decorative and hidden from assistive technology, so the name is what people hear.

Links sit side by side and wrap onto further rows on narrow screens. The links are marked up as a list.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-action-links"><code>civica-action-links</code></h3>

A custom card from the Civica companion module. It needs the module from version 1.1.0 loaded as a dashboard resource, in the same `civica-ui.js` file as the colour settings. Without the module the card type does not exist. See [Install](../get-started/install.html).

Add a manual card:

```yaml
type: custom:civica-action-links
links:
  - name: Housework
    path: /dashboard-housework/home
  - name: Lights
    path: '#lights'
  - name: Heating and hot water
    path: '#heating'
```

| Setting | Required | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `links` | Yes | A non-empty list of destinations |
| `links[].name` | Yes | Visible, meaningful text such as “Housework” |
| `links[].path` | Yes | A local path beginning with `/`, or a current-page fragment beginning with `#` and at least one more character |

Replace the examples with destinations that exist in your installation. Quote a fragment such as `'#lights'` so YAML does not treat the `#` as a comment. A fragment only opens a pop-up when your dashboard already provides one.

The card rejects external addresses, addresses that begin with `//`, backslashes, blank names and spaces inside paths. Use an encoded path when an address needs a space. Names are shown as text, never as HTML. If you replace a working configuration with an invalid one, the existing links stay. Configuration errors appear in Home Assistant’s card editor.

Home Assistant has no card of this kind. For navigation from another card, set `tap_action` to `navigate` with a `navigation_path`. For an external address, use a link in a Markdown card.

**What the theme controls.** These Home Assistant theme values change how the card looks. The colours below are for the default Civica theme. They follow the theme colour you choose: the other theme colours use a different brand colour (`civica-brand-primary`) for the circle tint, and their own link and hover colours.

| Setting | Light | Dark | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `link-color` | #1A65A6 | #C0D7EB | The link name |
| `civica-action-hover` | #195C96 | #D6E5F2 | Hover and pressed state |
| `civica-brand-primary` | #1D70B8 | #1D70B8 | A 20% tint of the circle |
| `primary-text-color` | #0B0C0C | #FFFFFF | The arrow |
| `civica-focus-text` and `civica-focus-background` | #0B0C0C and #FFDD00 | Same | The keyboard focus cue |
| `civica-font-size-body` | 19 px, scaled by Home Assistant’s font size setting | Same | The size of the link name |

**What does not change.** The name is always bold and underlined. The circle is 40 pixels with a 24 pixel arrow, fixed by the card. Visited and unvisited links look the same, because dashboard content changes often.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Real links: Enter activates the focused link, and the context menu, modifier keys and back history work as in any browser.
- Targets are at least 44 pixels high, larger than the 24 pixel minimum.
- Navigation stays in the same tab and can reload Home Assistant. The card does not use Home Assistant’s own navigation function. A fragment link uses the browser’s own hash navigation, which is what dashboard pop-ups listen for.
- Hovering or pressing a link thickens its underline and changes its colour.
- It cannot link outside Home Assistant, run a service or create a pop-up.
- There is no disabled, pending, success or error state. Every item is a working link.
- It needs the companion module. Without it, the card type is not available.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Keep the group short, usually two to five links. Write a verb-led, specific name: “Open heating schedule”, not “Click here”. Use the same name for the same destination everywhere. Never link to an action that surprises people, such as turning something off.

Use a link to go somewhere and a button to do something. See [Links](../styles/typography-links.html) for the underline and colour rules.

Success criteria that apply:

- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context) and 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: the name says where the link goes.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the underline marks the link, not colour alone.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): focus uses yellow and ink, also while hovered.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): 44 pixel targets.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.4 Resize Text: links wrap at 320 CSS pixels.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the group is a list.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the arrow is hidden, so the name is announced once.
- 3.2.4 Consistent Identification: the same name for the same destination.

In forced-colours mode the card uses system colours. Check the real destination, and any pop-up, in your live dashboard before you rely on it.

